{
  "id": "set-matrix-zeroes",
  "questionId": "set-matrix-zeroes",
  "slug": "set-matrix-zeroes",
  "title": "Set Matrix Zeroes",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Two pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "matrix manipulation",
    "in-place marking"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you can't zero cells as you find them during the first pass, or you'll cause a zero to spread further than it should - so where can you record 'this row/column needs zeroing' without a full second grid?",
    "what if the first row and first column of the matrix itself served as your marker arrays for every other row and column?",
    "before overwriting the first row/column as markers, remember separately whether the first row and first column originally contained a zero themselves",
    "do a pass setting matrix[i][0] and matrix[0][j] to 0 whenever matrix[i][j] is 0, then a second pass zeroing every cell whose marker says so, then finally handle row 0 and column 0 using what you remembered"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "zeroing cells during the same pass used to detect zeroes, causing newly-zeroed cells to trigger more zeroing than intended",
    "forgetting to separately track whether row 0 or column 0 originally had a zero, when using them as marker storage",
    "using O(rows * cols) extra space with a full boolean copy of the grid instead of a smaller row/column marker set"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a grid of integers.\n\n**Whenever a cell contains 0, set its entire row and its entire column to 0, and return the resulting grid.**\n\nDo this for every zero in the original grid at once, don't let zeroes you create along the way trigger more zeroing.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= rows, cols <= 50",
      "-1000 <= matrix[i][j] <= 1000",
      "matrix is rectangular"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "no zeroes anywhere in the input",
      "the entire matrix is zeroes already",
      "a single row or single column matrix",
      "a zero in the first row or first column (easy to mix up with any 'use the first row/column as markers' optimization)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "matrix = [[1,1,1],[1,0,1],[1,1,1]]",
        "output": "[[1,0,1],[0,0,0],[1,0,1]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1,0,1],[0,0,0],[1,0,1]]",
        "explanation": "the single 0 at the center wipes out its whole row and whole column"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "matrix = [[0,1,2,0],[3,4,5,2],[1,3,1,5]]",
        "output": "[[0,0,0,0],[0,4,5,0],[0,3,1,0]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[0,0,0,0],[0,4,5,0],[0,3,1,0]]",
        "explanation": "two zeroes in row 0 wipe out row 0 entirely, plus columns 0 and 3 everywhere"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "matrix = [[1,2],[3,4]]",
        "output": "[[1,2],[3,4]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1,2],[3,4]]",
        "explanation": "no zeroes in the input, so nothing changes"
      }
    ],
